(1018390, 120153, 20941, '<td valign="top">Wow! Another wonderful ribbon shot! I seem to recall in the debate re masters challenges you felt it was partly luck that earned some of your ribbons...well it isn''t. Your ability to ribbon in different catagories with varying style is talent. We''ll be seeing you in the ribbons again soon! Congratulations.</td>', 'Wow! Another wonderful ribbon shot! I seem to recall in the debate re masters challenges you felt it was partly luck that earned some of your ribbons...well it isn''t. Your ability to ribbon in different catagories with varying style is talent. We''ll be seeing you in the ribbons again soon! Congratulations.', '2004-11-17 21:09:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(1018509, 118629, 12362, '<td valign="top">AMAZING shot Yanik.<br/> <br/>From the second that I saw this I knew it would bring home a blue.<br/>Fantastic last minute idea.</td>', 'AMAZING shot Yanik. From the second that I saw this I knew it would bring home a blue.Fantastic last minute idea.', '2004-11-17 21:44:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(1018511, 119920, 29168, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all your comments.<br/> <br/>I''m embarrased about the frame. It really does flatten out the contrast! Thing is, I put that on to <i>enhance</i> the contrast on a <i>black</i>, full screen display without a second thought that the DPC background is neutral grey. Doh! Foolish of me. &lt;sigh&gt; <br/> <br/>In hindsight, the crop should have been much tighter at the mercy of the symetry I had aimed for while taking the shot. That might have given it more of a macro feel - but I was working on the "or at least extreme close-up" aspect of the challenge description. (That "rock" is a piece of wood about two inches tall. The foreground tree is a one inch high weed clipping.) The image as a whole does not make much of that obvious. A flaw to be sure.<br/> <br/>"Fractal" was about the shapes. The shape of the house''s roof and that of the "rock", the shapes of the tiny foreground tree and the large background tree (and that they are both "trees".) <br/> <br/>I think I tried too hard on this. I was trying to get too much out of one picture. I should have just let it flow I think. The "KISS" principal maybe.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-12-06 21:54:40.</i></td>', 'Thanks for all your comments. I''m embarrased about the frame. It really does flatten out the contrast! Thing is, I put that on to enhance the contrast on a black, full screen display without a second thought that the DPC background is neutral grey. Doh! Foolish of me. <sigh>  In hindsight, the crop should have been much tighter at the mercy of the symetry I had aimed for while taking the shot. That might have given it more of a macro feel - but I was working on the "or at least extreme close-up" aspect of the challenge description. (That "rock" is a piece of wood about two inches tall. The foreground tree is a one inch high weed clipping.) The image as a whole does not make much of that obvious. A flaw to be sure. "Fractal" was about the shapes. The shape of the house''s roof and that of the "rock", the shapes of the tiny foreground tree and the large background tree (and that they are both "trees".)  I think I tried too hard on this. I was trying to get too much out of one picture. I should have just let it flow I think. The "KISS" principal maybe.', '2004-11-17 21:45:16.000000', '2004-12-06 21:54:40.000000', 0),
(1018551, 119255, 27574, '<td valign="top">I am tickled to come in 15th...this is only my second Challenge and I am learning my way around.  I figure this is the best place to say "Thank You" to everyone who took the time to comment...I really appreciate every single comment.  When I vote now...I take the time more often to comment on photos that I really like or that you can tell the person really put their heart into.<br/> <br/>Thanks again...am off to vote some more, whew....more than 600 this time.</td>', 'I am tickled to come in 15th...this is only my second Challenge and I am learning my way around.  I figure this is the best place to say "Thank You" to everyone who took the time to comment...I really appreciate every single comment.  When I vote now...I take the time more often to comment on photos that I really like or that you can tell the person really put their heart into. Thanks again...am off to vote some more, whew....more than 600 this time.', '2004-11-17 22:01:39.000000', NULL, 0),
